    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Trea Turner** (75.89) standing as our clear anchor, buoyed by a favorable matchup against Dollander and, crucially, a slow opposing pitcher delivery that maximizes his elite SB potential. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (72.94) also projects as an elite play; despite batting eighth, his 100% SB efficiency rating against Rocker makes him a must-start in our scarcity-focused build. **Kyle Schwarber** (69.69) rounds out our top-tier targets, leveraging a high xwOBA and a prime #2 lineup spot against the same Dollander matchup, providing excellent volume for HR and RBI production in a favorable park.\n\nWhile the core is strong, we are forced to navigate some tactical volatility. **Yordan Alvarez** (57.92) and **Junior Caminero** (56.98) sit in the marginal-to-strong transition zone; **Yordan Alvarez** faces a difficult L/L matchup against Lodolo, curbing his ceiling, while **Junior Caminero** contends with a tough 3.03 xERA arm. Our most difficult decision was the benching of **Hyeseong Kim** (36.57). While his raw profile is appealing, the optimizer prioritized the platoon stability of our starters over **Hyeseong Kim**, who faces a high-level threat in Sale, significantly suppressing his floor and making him a liability in a daily-cap environment. \n\nManagers should keep a close watch on the **Gary Sanchez** lineup announcement. Currently, he is slotted at #3 based on projected data, but any deviation downward in the batting order would shift his 50.24 score toward the \"Below floor\" designation. Meanwhile, we continue to monitor the status of **Christian Yelich**, **Colt Keith**, and **Zac Veen**, none of whom are available to provide support for our current depth chart. We are leaning heavily into speed-positive matchups today, hoping the high-efficiency baserunning of **Trea Turner** and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** compensates for the harder-to-project outcomes from **Caleb Durbin** and **Chase Meidroth**.",
